after days of uncertainty the security  council renewed permission for  humanitarian aid to flow from turkey  into syria but for just six months  it was deeply controversial the uk  france and the us abstained a resolution  with a 12-month timeline which is what  the un and aid agencies requested was  defeated on friday when russia used its  veto the vote we took this morning is  what happens  when one council member takes the entire  security council  hostage  with the lives of syrian men women and  children hanging in the balance  the resolution that was finally passed  was very similar to the one russia had  itself proposed on friday then 13 out of  15 council members had voted against it  norway and ireland introduced the new  version look first of all this was not a  decision taken on the basis of a russian  draft today this was a co-pen holder  russia ireland and norway  draft just a few words different i think  on the face of it you may look at that  and say that james but you know that the  devil is in the detail and i think if  you look at the checks there are  significant differences  what is different is a clause requiring  the un secretary general antonio  guterres to provide a report on the aid  situation by december he gave me his  reaction to the new resolution we have  been asking for the renewal of  that  cross border  point  as it is essential for  the people in italy it's a matter of  life and death for many of them  we have asked for a renewal of  one year  the security council approved six months  but i strongly hope that after this six  months  it will be renewed the head of the human  rights group amnesty international says  the events of recent days show the work  of the security council is now virtually  paralyzed we do need a different system  the security council has run as is  clearly demonstrating that it has run  the end of its path right now something  else need to come up  with just a sixth month extension  members of the security council will  have to renegotiate humanitarian aid in  syria in january that's a time of year  when conditions are particularly tough  for people in northern syria because of  the harsh wintry weather james bay's al  jazeera at the united nations well cena  kosovo has just returned from the turkey  syria border she says ngos working there  believe the veto power of the security  council remains a threat to the delivery  of aid  extending the u.n humanitarian aid  delivery to northern syria for six more  months may ease the tension for now but  it is not seen as a solution  international and local ngos have  already been warning that planning and  implementing such a wide-scale operation  into northwestern syria where 4.5  million civilians reside requires longer  than 6 months time  there are 4.4 million civilians just in  idlib and among all of them 2.4 million  of them depend on international  humanitarian aid that's why they say six  months time will not be a solution to  their problems as the children in idlib  are facing a big risk of hunger and they  are already undernourished and the  majority of the population are children  and women which is seen as the  vulnerable segment of the society some  of the ngos already started showing  their reaction saying that world is just  watching what's going on in idlib and  some of them say that humanitarian  actions should be taken away from u.n  security council as russia's veto is  always a threat for the civilians an  alternative b plan must be established  and local grassroots ngos inside syria  must be empowered  let's speak to matthew bryza who's a  board member of jamestown foundation and  a former american diplomat he's joining  us from cacao welcome to al jazeera once  again so what is your reaction to this  resolution that has now passed that  allows cross-border aid deliveries into  syria and to the rebel-held region for  for six months as opposed to a year  well my first reaction is thank goodness  uh second reaction is yeah russia won  this round of diplomacy um not that it  really matters who won or lost but you  know russia instrumentalizes  uh  the rest of the world's desire to do  humanitarian good uh and in this case it  got a second bite and a third bite at  the apple uh by now only having a  six-month renewal so that six months  from now  russia can try to extract something else  from the international community when  the rest of us are totally desperate in  winter to get that such badly needed  humanitarian aid to those people in  northwest syria but were there any  major differences in the resolution that  russia had put forward just a few days  ago in which  a lot of countries were against and then  there comes this one  um  and it passed i mean we heard from our  reporter james baze at the u.n um  questioning this bill matt saying well  there isn't that much difference in the  resolution so why did it pass now  yeah darren i think james bay's got it  exactly right russia was playing a game  of chicken with the international  community uh it knew that it could force  through its draft uh and the draft that  it wants uh wants basically  to make it more difficult for  humanitarian assistance to get into  northwest syria without the help of the  assad regime uh and so it passed now  because the rest of the world realized  we have to get this aid uh to these the  people who are suffering in northwest  syria so so the rest of the world  blinked and that really meant you know  the united states united kingdom and  friends on the security council uh it's  it's a brutal tactic by russia and i  experienced it when i was still with the  us government uh every year we had to  renew a u.n peacekeeping operation in  georgia  after which or during which russia  invaded and occupied northwest georgia  and russia prevented the provision of  humanitarian assistance to georgians in  the areas that russia had invaded and  occupied uh as a negotiating tool just  like it's doing right now let me just  play devil's advocate for a moment i  mean russia says that it wants to see  the aids come through damascus as you  just said also through the government in  damascus and it insists that you know  this this route violates syrian  sovereignty does russia not have a point  russia definitely has a point there and  the difference is that the rest of us at  least on the security council consider  humanitarian aid uh an end in itself  something that should not be politicized  something that should not be used as a  way to shape the politics on the ground  uh but having said that i mean going  back to the georgia example we much  preferred meaning we being the security  council uh permanent representatives the  united states france and the uk that  that assistance would have come from the  georgian government to the separatist  regions uh rather than from in that case  russia so yeah russia russia has a point  but it also has a different philosophy  it's perfectly willing to  instrumentalize the humanitarian  suffering uh to secure its political  objectives let me just ask you uh just  to give us your thoughts on the  divisions amongst the members of the  security council and particularly uh i  mean there have always been divisions as  we know but particularly these days the  in the wake of of course the the war  taking place in ukraine i mean how would  you describe relations among security  council members right now at present  ah yeah poisonous with regard to russia  versus again the permanent members uh  from the west uk france and united  states uh and it's interesting that by  the way uh china abstained uh on the  resolution a couple of days ago that  that russia vetoed so yeah and china of  course is a member of the p5 uh china  realizes that it's not doing itself any  favors if it too like russia tries to  instrumentalize humanitarian assistance  so relations are really bad in the  security council and you know as as the  french representative was saying in your  package uh the the security council  mechanism really is is broken it doesn't  work now when you've got russia willing  to veto anything that france the uk and  the u.s want and vice versa yeah all  right thank you so much uh matthew prize  ever speaking to us from cacao thank you
 

